            "The <strong>WHO analgesic ladder</strong> has three steps. <strong>Step one</strong>, for mild pain, is <strong>paracetamol or a non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug</strong>; <strong>step two</strong>, for moderate pain or pain not relieved by step one, adds a <strong>weak opioid — codeine or tramadol</strong> — to the step-one drug; <strong>step three</strong>, for severe pain, replaces the weak opioid with a <strong>strong opioid, morphine</strong>, still with the step-one drug. The patient with cancer pain not relieved by regular paracetamol and codeine moves to <strong>morphine</strong>, not to a different weak opioid or a bigger dose of codeine. The ladder is climbed according to the pain, not according to how ill the patient looks or how long they have left; analgesia is given <strong>by the clock</strong> to keep pain away rather than chasing it when it returns; the side effect is prescribed with the drug (a laxative with the first dose of morphine); and <strong>adjuvants</strong> are added for specific pains — amitriptyline, gabapentin or carbamazepine for neuropathic pain, dexamethasone for nerve compression and raised intracranial pressure, hyoscine butylbromide for colic, diazepam for muscle spasm. Pain is assessed with a score (0–10, faces for children, behavioural scales for the non-verbal) before and after every dose, and the score is the 'right response' that tells the nurse whether the ladder needs climbing.",
