        "The WHO ladder is climbed by the pain — paracetamol or an NSAID, then add codeine or tramadol, then morphine with no ceiling dose — given by the clock with the step-one drug kept and a laxative prescribed with the first dose of morphine; adjuvants treat neuropathic and specific pains; paracetamol is capped at 4 g a day and overdose is treated with acetylcysteine even in the patient who feels well; NSAIDs are avoided in ulcer disease, kidney disease, dehydration, heart failure, late pregnancy and aspirin-sensitive asthma.",
